That's a classic double-submit race condition. The fix is to disable the button optimistically **and** guard the handler itself, not just the UI:

```javascript
let isSubmitting = false;

async function handlePay() {
  if (isSubmitting) return;
  isSubmitting = true;
  payButton.disabled = true;

  try {
    await chargeCart();
  } finally {
    isSubmitting = false;
    payButton.disabled = false;
  }
}
```

⚠️ Disabling the button alone isn't enough — a fast second click can fire before React re-renders. The `isSubmitting` flag is the real guard.

The most common cause is an object or array in the dependency array that gets a new reference every render:

```javascript
// ❌ options is a new object every render — effect fires every time
const options = { limit: 10 };
useEffect(() => { fetchData(options); }, [options]);
```

```javascript
// ✅ move it outside, or memoize it
const options = useMemo(() => ({ limit: 10 }), []);
useEffect(() => { fetchData(options); }, [options]);
```

If `fetchData` is defined inside the component, yes — ESLint's `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` rule will flag this. Wrap it in `useCallback` so it doesn't get a new identity every render either:

```javascript
const fetchData = useCallback(async (opts) => {
  const res = await api.get('/items', { params: opts });
  setItems(res.data);
}, []); // deps of fetchData itself

useEffect(() => { fetchData(options); }, [fetchData, options]);
```

